    <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/knowledge/">Knowledge</a> › Supplier checklist</p>
    <h1>China peptide supplier checklist: 12 points to verify</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">A supplier evaluation framework for buyers who cannot visit the factory — which is most buyers.</p>

    <h2>The 12 points</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Check</th><th>What evidence looks like</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>Legal entity</td><td>Business licence matching the bank account and invoice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>Product scope</td><td>Consistent catalogue with real specifications, not copied text</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>Batch COA</td><td>Lot-specific, with chromatogram and MS</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>Analytical methods</td><td>Column, gradient, wavelength, instrument</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>Traceability</td><td>Batch number linking vial, COA and production record</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6</td><td>Retention samples</td><td>Stated retention period and storage</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7</td><td>Deviation handling</td><td>Written OOS procedure; non-conforming lots not shipped</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>Certifications</td><td>Verifiable certificate numbers and scope — or no claim</td></tr>
        <tr><td>9</td><td>Export compliance</td><td>Accurate declarations; restricted-destination policy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10</td><td>Commercial terms</td><td>Written quotation, Incoterm, payment terms, lead time</td></tr>
        <tr><td>11</td><td>Communication</td><td>Technical replies, not sales scripts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12</td><td>Consistency</td><td>Two or three consecutive lots with comparable data</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <p>Score each point 0–2. Below 18/24, treat the supplier as unqualified for regulated or repeat business. The point is not to find a perfect supplier — it is to make the gaps visible before money moves.</p>

    <h2>The most common failure</h2>
    <p>Point 3 and point 5 fail together: suppliers provide a generic COA and cannot link it to the lot shipped. If that happens, no other strength compensates.</p>

    <div class="ruo">Research use only. This framework is commercial due diligence, not regulatory certification.</div>

